WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:06.945
A meter has to keep measuring, and keep its record, even when the mains is gone.

00:00:06.945 --> 00:00:12.385
SKU-2 puts measurement, security and an always-on clock on one die.

00:00:13.435 --> 00:00:17.037
Voltage and current flow into six 24-bit converters.

00:00:17.037 --> 00:00:22.231
They set billing accuracy, so they are dedicated hardware, calibrated once.

00:00:23.281 --> 00:00:30.577
A decimator filters each raw bit stream into clean samples, so the processor never touches the measurement chain.

00:00:31.627 --> 00:00:36.286
What the meter charges for is computed right beside the converters.

00:00:36.286 --> 00:00:40.736
Real, reactive and apparent power, and harmonics up to the 15th.

00:00:41.786 --> 00:00:45.180
Results cross the bus to be stored and signed.

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:51.895
Secure boot and tamper key wiping protect the code, and the keys, every reading depends on.

00:00:52.945 --> 00:00:59.275
Readings leave on the meter's own protocol, beside the display, the relay and the LEDs.

00:00:59.275 --> 00:01:01.530
One die covers the whole meter.

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:10.516
An always-on domain keeps the clock and the tamper log alive on a backup cell, drawing under 2 microwatts.

00:01:11.566 --> 00:01:13.323
SKU-2 is pre-silicon.

00:01:13.323 --> 00:01:15.414
Every figure is a target.

00:01:15.414 --> 00:01:23.530
Accuracy over a fifteen-year life, and leakage through a thirty-day outage, must still be proven.

00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:27.880
Bring us your accuracy class and your outage budget.

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:31.143
We will tell you what SKU-2 must prove.
